1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,645
A young couple move into a crowded street.

2
00:00:02,645 --> 00:00:10,705
On one side, a Macedonian family who shout so loudly that for six months the newcomers think they are murdering each other.

3
00:00:10,705 --> 00:00:11,642
They are not.

4
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:12,481
They are just talking.

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:16,230
Nobody in this street shares a language with the couple.

6
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:22,011
Yet within one year, these strangers will be cheering at dawn for a baby that is not theirs.

7
00:00:22,011 --> 00:00:22,353
How?

8
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:34,459
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 1: Neighbours, a very short story by the Australian writer Tim Winton.

9
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:37,928
Six parts.

10
00:00:37,928 --> 00:00:39,881
The writer and his Australia.

11
00:00:39,881 --> 00:00:42,121
The story, season by season.

12
00:00:42,121 --> 00:00:43,840
What crosses the fence.

13
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:45,077
The characters.

14
00:00:45,077 --> 00:00:46,275
The themes.

15
00:00:46,275 --> 00:00:50,652
And how the story is told: point of view, structure and devices.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,379
Tim Winton was born in nineteen sixty in Perth and grew up in Western Australia.

17
00:01:00,379 --> 00:01:08,491
He published his first novel at twenty two, and has since won Australia's top fiction prize, the Miles Franklin Award, four times.

18
00:01:08,491 --> 00:01:13,310
Neighbours comes from his first collection of stories, Scission, from nineteen eighty five.

19
00:01:13,533 --> 00:01:16,009
Three things about his writing matter here.

20
00:01:16,009 --> 00:01:18,118
He writes about ordinary people.

21
00:01:18,118 --> 00:01:25,930
His prose is plain and physical: smells, sounds, actions, almost never a stated feeling.

22
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:28,987
And place does the emotional work, like a character.

23
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:41,998
The story is set in the nineteen eighties, when Australia had officially adopted multiculturalism: many cultures accepted side by side, rather than migrants being expected to melt into the majority.

24
00:01:41,998 --> 00:01:47,415
Families from southern and eastern Europe had long settled in the cheaper inner suburbs.

25
00:01:47,415 --> 00:01:54,673
Winton's street, with Macedonian, Polish, Italian and Greek neighbours, is what that policy looked like on the ground.

26
00:01:59,467 --> 00:02:02,359
Almost nothing dramatic happens in Neighbours.

27
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:05,159
There is no villain and no crisis.

28
00:02:05,159 --> 00:02:09,872
What changes is a relationship, and Winton measures it in seasons.

29
00:02:09,872 --> 00:02:11,067
Let us walk through the year.

30
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:14,669
They arrive wary.

31
00:02:14,669 --> 00:02:19,942
Raised in quiet outer suburbs, they feel like visitors in a foreign land.

32
00:02:19,942 --> 00:02:28,966
The Macedonians shout, the old Polish widower hammers nails into wood and pulls them out again, and a little boy urinates in the street.

33
00:02:28,966 --> 00:02:36,534
The disapproval runs both ways: the neighbours frown at a wife who goes out to work while the husband stays home writing a thesis.

34
00:02:37,767 --> 00:02:44,201
In autumn they dig a vegetable garden, and the garden brings the neighbours to the fence with advice.

35
00:02:44,201 --> 00:02:50,021
The young man resents it, but, tellingly, he takes careful note of what is said.

36
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:58,108
His wife is bolder: she runs a hand over the little boy's shaved head, and the big woman next door gives her garlic to plant.

37
00:02:59,333 --> 00:03:02,772
They build a henhouse, and the neighbours watch it fall down.

38
00:03:02,772 --> 00:03:08,723
Then the old Polish widower slips through the fence, uninvited, and rebuilds it for them.

39
00:03:08,723 --> 00:03:13,957
Winton adds one flat sentence: they could not understand a word he said.

40
00:03:13,957 --> 00:03:15,273
That is the heart of the story.

41
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,002
In winter, gifts start crossing.

42
00:03:19,002 --> 00:03:23,442
The couple give away cabbages and receive grappa and firewood.

43
00:03:23,442 --> 00:03:26,111
They no longer walk with their eyes lowered.

44
00:03:26,111 --> 00:03:36,039
And when their own parents visit and look shocked at the neighbours, the couple feel superior and proud, which shows how far they have come, and that a little pride is still there.

45
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:42,438
In spring the Macedonian family teach them to slaughter and pluck their ducks.

46
00:03:42,438 --> 00:03:49,404
Everyone sits on buckets telling barely understood stories, and the couple feel almost drunk with it.

47
00:03:49,404 --> 00:03:55,140
By the end they are shouting too, behaving exactly like the neighbours whose noise once appalled them.

48
00:03:56,367 --> 00:04:00,236
Then an unplanned pregnancy, and the street takes it over.

49
00:04:00,236 --> 00:04:09,533
The deli man presses chocolates on her, Italian women offer names, Greek women feel her belly, and the woman next door knits a baby suit.

50
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:15,027
The Pole finally builds with his wood: a two car garage, though he has no car.

51
00:04:15,027 --> 00:04:21,821
Just as the young man is about to complain about the noise, the old man arrives with a barrowful of firewood for them.

52
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:25,574
The baby comes at home, through the night.

53
00:04:25,574 --> 00:04:30,391
Fetching wood at dusk, the young man counts twelve faces at the fences.

54
00:04:30,391 --> 00:04:37,449
A boy is born at dawn, and on the Macedonian side a queue of sleepy faces looks up and cheers.

55
00:04:37,449 --> 00:04:38,777
He begins to weep.

56
00:04:38,777 --> 00:04:43,601
The twentieth century novel, he realises, had not prepared him for this.

57
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:54,415
Here is the best way to answer the exam question, what happens in this story.

58
00:04:54,415 --> 00:04:55,782
Do not say nothing.

59
00:04:55,782 --> 00:05:01,563
Say the plot is a change of relationship, and prove it with the things that cross the fences.

60
00:05:01,563 --> 00:05:02,696
Advice.

61
00:05:02,696 --> 00:05:03,516
Garlic.

62
00:05:03,516 --> 00:05:05,118
A rebuilt henhouse.

63
00:05:05,118 --> 00:05:09,792
Cabbages going one way, grappa and firewood coming back.

64
00:05:09,792 --> 00:05:11,758
Lessons in killing ducks.

65
00:05:11,758 --> 00:05:12,982
A knitted suit.

66
00:05:12,982 --> 00:05:14,323
A barrow of wood.

67
00:05:14,323 --> 00:05:15,969
And finally, a cheer.

68
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:21,853
Notice: not a single line of dialogue crosses those fences in the whole story.

69
00:05:21,853 --> 00:05:24,950
Everything that matters is an object or an action.

70
00:05:29,733 --> 00:05:30,946
The characters.

71
00:05:30,946 --> 00:05:42,417
Watch the young man's verbs in the first half: he senses disapproval, resents advice, assumes why the boy's head is shaved, suspects the old man of annoying him on purpose.

72
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:46,154
He reads the street like a text, from a distance.

73
00:05:46,154 --> 00:05:54,757
His wife has fewer lines but more courage: she makes the first friendly touch, and at the birth Winton gives her all the heroic words.

74
00:05:54,967 --> 00:05:59,656
The Polish widower is the most moving figure and the one we know least.

75
00:05:59,656 --> 00:06:05,711
He rebuilds their henhouse and brings them firewood, and they never understand a word he says.

76
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:11,958
And the Macedonian family begin as noise and end as a choir, cheering at the fence.

77
00:06:13,167 --> 00:06:15,760
Notice that nobody in the story has a name.

78
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:20,747
A name is something someone tells you, and across these fences nobody can.

79
00:06:20,747 --> 00:06:22,557
So the narrator withholds them.

80
00:06:22,557 --> 00:06:33,301
This makes the story representative, any couple in any migrant street, and it makes the ending stronger: these people behave like family without ever having been introduced.

81
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:39,286
Four themes.

82
00:06:39,286 --> 00:06:45,458
First, community across language: people can belong to each other without sharing words.

83
00:06:45,458 --> 00:06:48,831
Second, prejudice and its slow correction.

84
00:06:48,831 --> 00:07:02,333
The couple's prejudice is not hatred; it is the mild, educated, superior kind, the hardest kind to see in yourself, and Winton corrects it with time and closeness, not with an argument.

85
00:07:02,333 --> 00:07:06,141
He is fair, too: the neighbours judge the couple as well.

86
00:07:06,367 --> 00:07:09,936
Third, book learning against lived experience.

87
00:07:09,936 --> 00:07:18,218
The young man studies the modern novel, famous for loneliness and irony, while the real lesson happens outside his window.

88
00:07:18,218 --> 00:07:24,342
Fourth, birth as a shared, physical event: one baby keeps a whole street awake.

89
00:07:25,567 --> 00:07:30,178
And the last line settles the contest between books and life.

90
00:07:30,178 --> 00:07:33,928
The twentieth century novel had not prepared him for this.

91
00:07:33,928 --> 00:07:39,188
The man who studies literature is educated by a street he once looked down on.

92
00:07:39,188 --> 00:07:43,343
That is irony, and it is the story's whole argument in a sentence.

93
00:07:48,133 --> 00:07:49,176
How is it told?

94
00:07:49,176 --> 00:08:01,208
In the third person, but limited almost entirely to the young man: a technique called free indirect style, where the narrator's words take on a character's attitudes without quotation marks.

95
00:08:01,208 --> 00:08:02,718
Read this sentence.

96
00:08:02,718 --> 00:08:12,015
The guess about the shaved head is his, not the narrator's, and the little words he assumed warn us that he is inventing reasons for people he does not know.

97
00:08:12,015 --> 00:08:18,216
Because we see through his eyes, our own prejudice is corrected at the same speed as his.

98
00:08:19,433 --> 00:08:20,502
The structure.

99
00:08:20,502 --> 00:08:25,073
There are no chapters or scene breaks; the seasons are the structure.

100
00:08:25,073 --> 00:08:31,765
The garden is planted in autumn, and the child is conceived in autumn too; both come to harvest at the end.

101
00:08:31,765 --> 00:08:35,493
That parallel is why such a plotless story feels complete.

102
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:36,903
The devices to quote. Understatement: they could not understand a word he said, so flat that the kindness feels enormous. Irony in the last line. Sensory imagery, sunsets and wood smoke, instead of named feelings. Lists, including a list of mixed feelings. Symbols: the fence, the lumber, the garden. And a motif of sound that runs from shouting to a cheer.

103
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:42,504
Pause and think.

104
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:57,194
Names are learned by talking, and nobody can talk across these fences; it also makes the story stand for any migrant street.

105
00:08:59,167 --> 00:08:59,602
One more.

106
00:09:07,233 --> 00:09:12,491
The Polish widower rebuilds their henhouse, and they cannot understand a word he says.

107
00:09:14,667 --> 00:09:15,749
Quick recap.

108
00:09:15,749 --> 00:09:19,512
Winton and the multicultural Australia of the eighties.

109
00:09:19,512 --> 00:09:22,715
One year in one street, season by season.

110
00:09:22,715 --> 00:09:25,749
The plot as the things that cross the fence.

111
00:09:25,749 --> 00:09:29,786
Themes of community, prejudice, books and life.

112
00:09:29,786 --> 00:09:36,297
And the telling: free indirect style, the seasons, no names, and an ironic last line.

113
00:09:40,467 --> 00:09:41,732
That was Neighbours.

114
00:09:41,732 --> 00:09:49,492
Next, a story about a woman, her husband's guest, and a feeling she will not name: A Respectable Woman.

115
00:09:49,492 --> 00:09:50,047
See you there.

